WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.233
Regression testing for prompts and models.

2
00:00:03.533 --> 00:00:06.200
You wouldn't merge code that fails its tests.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.333
Prompts and models deserve the same.

4
00:00:09.533 --> 00:00:11.333
This is evals-first engineering.

5
00:00:11.633 --> 00:00:16.733
Every prompt tweak, model upgrade, or retrieval change runs the full eval suite.

6
00:00:17.033 --> 00:00:20.067
And the score diff justifies the change, or blocks it.

7
00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:22.567
Compare the two habits.

8
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:24.814
By feel, you try three examples.

9
00:00:24.814 --> 00:00:27.067
By evidence, you run the whole suite.

10
00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:28.726
One says it looks better.

11
00:00:28.726 --> 00:00:30.900
The other shows a score diff per metric.

12
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:37.400
This catches the silent killer: a change that improves what you watched while breaking ten cases you didn't.

13
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.767
Mechanically, it's a gate in CI.

14
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:43.633
A pull request changes a prompt or a model.

15
00:00:43.933 --> 00:00:46.967
CI runs the benchmark and the golden set on it.

16
00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:50.100
Each metric is compared to the baseline from main.

17
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.093
If a metric drops more than a tolerance, the build fails.

18
00:00:54.093 --> 00:00:57.333
Scores are noisy, so never gate on exact equality.

19
00:00:58.533 --> 00:01:00.367
The average isn't the whole story.

20
00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:03.875
Faithfulness looks fine here, but relevancy collapsed.

21
00:01:03.875 --> 00:01:07.500
The per-question diff names exactly what you're trading away.

22
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.700
Here's the gate as a workflow file.

23
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.967
It triggers on every pull request.

24
00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:15.400
One step scores the changed pipeline.

25
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.378
The next fails on a drop of more than zero point zero two.

26
00:01:19.378 --> 00:01:21.533
Red needs a very good explanation.

27
00:01:22.733 --> 00:01:24.033
Here's a real story.

28
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:27.225
A cheaper model arrives, forty percent less.

29
00:01:27.225 --> 00:01:29.000
You change one config line.

30
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:36.464
The diff shows relevancy fell from zero point eight six to zero point seven one, with nine questions named.

31
00:01:36.464 --> 00:01:37.000
Blocked.

32
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.792
One prompt line fixes it, and you merge.

33
00:01:39.792 --> 00:01:43.467
The safety net cost fifty five questions and three minutes.

34
00:01:44.667 --> 00:01:45.437
Let's recap.

35
00:01:45.437 --> 00:01:49.033
Run the full eval suite on every prompt or model change.

36
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:52.167
Gate on the score diff, with a tolerance for noise.

37
00:01:52.467 --> 00:01:55.167
Read the per-question diff, not only the average.

38
00:01:55.467 --> 00:01:57.681
Evidence replaces seems better.

39
00:01:57.681 --> 00:01:59.467
Next up: golden datasets.
